There is indeed a small literature in academic finance that has found that "quantity" or in this case passive fund flows affect asset prices in surprisingly big ways. I'm very familiar given that that was also the general topic of my PhD work, which was on the effect of supply on bond prices. Very little of the extant literature points to some catastrophic tail risk caused by the growing popularity of passive indexing, which is the additional step the likes of Mike Green have taken without seemingly understood the underlying literature except cherrypicking, twisting, and exaggerating parts of the findings that seemed confirmatory for their pre-determined conclusion that somehow the growing popularity of "passive investing", which they struggle to even define, is actually very risky and problematic. The whole thing is honestly tiresome and a total waste of everyone's time.

@choffstein right, I think we are on the same page. I was just referring to a very specific group of people trying to "catastrophize", as you put it, investors putting money mindlessly into SPY instead of conscientiously picking individual stocks and styles. 

